1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,018
PART 4 I'm going to report on a case study of a programme

2
00:00:08,018 --> 00:00:14,803
which has been set up to help rural populations in Mozambique,

3
00:00:14,803 --> 00:00:19,120
a largely agricultural country in South-East Africa.

4
00:00:19,120 --> 00:00:27,660
The programme worked with three communities in Chicualacuala district, near the Limpopo River.

5
00:00:27,660 --> 00:00:34,190
This is a dry and arid region, with unpredictable rainfall.

6
00:00:34,190 --> 00:00:41,669
Because of this, people in the area were unable to support themselves through agriculture

7
00:00:41,669 --> 00:00:51,820
and instead they used the forest as a means of providing themselves with an income, mainly by selling charcoal.

8
00:00:51,820 --> 00:00:59,100
However, this was not a sustainable way of living in the long term,

9
00:00:59,100 --> 00:01:03,580
as they were rapidly using up this resource.

10
00:01:03,580 --> 00:01:07,640
To support agriculture in this dry region,

11
00:01:07,640 --> 00:01:19,820
the programme focused primarily on making use of existing water resources from the Limpopo River by setting up systems of irrigation,

12
00:01:19,820 --> 00:01:26,200
which would provide a dependable water supply for crops and animals.

13
00:01:26,200 --> 00:01:36,000
The programme worked closely with the district government in order to find the best way of implementing this.

14
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:39,580
The region already had one farmers

15
00:01:39,880 --> 00:01:45,880
association, and it was decided to set up two more of these.

16
00:01:45,880 --> 00:01:54,134
These associations planned and carried out activities including water management, livestock breeding

17
00:01:54,134 --> 00:02:00,240
and agriculture, and it was notable that in general

18
00:02:00,240 --> 00:02:05,140
women formed the majority of the workforce.

19
00:02:05,140 --> 00:02:11,716
It was decided that in order to keep the crops safe from animals,

20
00:02:11,716 --> 00:02:17,020
both wild and domestic, special areas should be fenced off

21
00:02:17,020 --> 00:02:19,810
where the crops could be grown.

22
00:02:19,810 --> 00:02:24,797
The community was responsible for creating these fences,

23
00:02:24,797 --> 00:02:31,030
but the programme provided the necessary wire for making them.

24
00:02:31,030 --> 00:02:37,030
Once the area had been fenced off, it could be cultivated.

25
00:02:37,030 --> 00:02:40,640
The land was dug, so that vegetables

26
00:02:40,640 --> 00:02:45,894
and cereals appropriate to the climate could be grown,

27
00:02:45,894 --> 00:02:50,880
and the programme provided the necessary seeds for this.

28
00:02:50,880 --> 00:02:54,580
The programme also provided pumps

29
00:02:54,580 --> 00:03:00,770
so that water could be brought from the river in pipes to the fields.

30
00:03:00,770 --> 00:03:06,253
However, the labour was all provided by local people,

31
00:03:06,253 --> 00:03:16,000
and they also provided and put up the posts that supported the fences around the fields.

32
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:24,000
Once the programme had been set up, its development was monitored carefully.

33
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:32,780
The farmers were able to grow enough produce not just for their own needs, but also to sell.

34
00:03:32,780 --> 00:03:36,640
However, getting the produce to places

35
00:03:36,640 --> 00:03:41,734
where it could be marketed was sometimes a problem,

36
00:03:41,734 --> 00:03:47,107
as the farmers did not have access to transport,

37
00:03:47,107 --> 00:03:54,270
and this resulted in large amounts of produce, especially vegetables, being spoiled.

38
00:03:54,270 --> 00:03:58,763
This problem was discussed with the farmers' associations

39
00:03:58,763 --> 00:04:06,065
and it was decided that in order to prevent food from being spoiled,

40
00:04:06,065 --> 00:04:11,120
the farmers needed to learn techniques for its preservation.

41
00:04:11,120 --> 00:04:17,651
There was also an additional initiative that had not been originally planned,

42
00:04:17,651 --> 00:04:22,550
but which became a central feature of the programme.

43
00:04:22,550 --> 00:04:30,227
This was when farmers started to dig holes for tanks in the fenced-off areas

44
00:04:30,227 --> 00:04:35,600
and to fill these with water and use them for breeding fish

45
00:04:36,180 --> 00:04:39,550
an important source of protein.

46
00:04:39,550 --> 00:04:45,085
After a time, another suggestion was made by local people

47
00:04:45,085 --> 00:04:49,580
which hadn't been part of the programme's original proposal

48
00:04:49,580 --> 00:04:53,940
but which was also adopted later on.

49
00:04:53,940 --> 00:04:59,550
They decided to try setting up colonies of bees,

50
00:04:59,550 --> 00:05:07,030
which would provide honey both for their own consumption and to sell.

51
00:05:07,030 --> 00:05:12,450
So what lessons can be learned from this programme?

52
00:05:12,450 --> 00:05:17,903
First of all, it tells us that in dry, arid regions,

53
00:05:18,644 --> 00:05:23,335
if there is access to a reliable source of water

54
00:05:23,335 --> 00:05:28,000
there is great potential for the development of agriculture.

55
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:34,404
In Chicualacuala, there was a marked improvement in agricultural production,

56
00:05:34,404 --> 00:05:44,650
which improved food security and benefited local people by providing them with both food and income.

57
00:05:44,650 --> 00:05:52,602
However, it's important to set realistic timelines for each phase of the programme,

58
00:05:52,602 --> 00:06:03,000
especially for its design, as mistakes made at this stage may be hard to correct later on.

59
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:20,000
The programme demonstrates that sustainable development is possible in areas where...
